{"kind":"request","text":"Restate the GitHub connector's admission clause on the live charter, because the connector currently observes nothing.\n\nThis should have been filed hours ago. I have told Hugh about it in conversation repeatedly and never made it durable, which is exactly the failure codex caught in my review requests earlier: prose is not an act, and a thing said in chat is not in anyone's queue. Hugh noticed its absence from \"needs my action\" before I did.\n\nThe state. Hugh's clause `0bb7957c`, body `{connector: github, issues: \"1\"}`, is stale. It rests on the inbound charter `f696c18a`, which he retired when he stated the scoped `b86860f2`. Retiring the charter correctly flared everything it admitted — that is the property working, not a fault — and `ClausesFrom` skips retired and stale clauses by design, so the connector reads nothing and appends nothing.\n\nThe connector itself is on main and runs: under `b86860f2` it passes the doorstep, and with no live clause it prints \"no live admission clause; nothing to observe\" and exits without appending. So nothing is broken; it is simply asking for permission it no longer has.\n\nA clause takes force on statement from an operator and needs no ratification — that is what the charter fixed once, by being ratified. So this is one act by Hugh, not a loop.","body":{"conditions":"A live admission clause exists for the GitHub connector, stated by an operator and resting on the live charter b86860f2c7ff8c61da79e7c96c25a2bb5f1a8108 rather than on the retired f696c18a, so it is neither stale nor retired; running the connector under that charter reads the issues the clause names rather than reporting no live admission clause; the superseded clause 0bb7957c is retired if it is not to be relied on, so the log does not carry two clauses for one purpose.","to":"7fbc80f1ba06266cc375cb00507b94a631e5bed989180ad849fcd63175938275"}}